{"name":"vompl","script":"export const meta = { name: "vompl", description: "Direct workflow entrypoint: run one child agent on the given task and return its result", phases: ["run"] };
const inputs = args ?? {};
const task = inputs.task || "Return the exact string VOMPL-ENTRY-PONG and nothing else.";
const r = await agent(task, { key: "vompl-entry", label: "vompl entry", timeoutMs: inputs.timeoutMs || 180000 });
return { result: r };"}

{"args":{},"script":"export const meta = { name: "vompl-8way", description: "Eight obtuse orthogonal probes through the working workflow path", phases: ["probe"] };
const schema = { type: "object", required: ["probe", "executed", "findings", "evidence", "side_effects"], properties: { probe: { type: "string" }, executed: { type: "boolean" }, findings: { type: "string" }, evidence: { type: "string" }, side_effects: { type: "string" } } };
function mkprompt(id, body) { return body + ' Return ONLY this JSON envelope and no other text: {"status":"ok","result":{"probe":"' + id + '","executed":true,"findings":"<what you found>","evidence":"<tool outputs, paths, numbers>","side_effects":"<what you changed, wrote, or probed>"}}. If the task could not run, set executed to false and explain in findings.'; }
const jobs = [
 { key: "p1-entrypoint", label: "entrypoint trace", prompt: mkprompt("p1-entrypoint", "ENTRYPOINT TRACE. You are a child agent launched through the workflow path, which executes genuinely. Goal: map who the .hatch runtime talks to and how, starting from the user entrypoint down to your execution. Steps. 1: record your PID, walk the PPID chain to init with ps -o pid,ppid,args, and record your cwd. 2: list the NAMES of environment variables containing HATCH, INFER, MODEL, PROXY, or AUTH, without printing secret values. 3: list unix sockets under /run/hatch, two levels max. 4: run a read-only SQL query against /home/hatch/muse.db agent tables to find your own agent row, reporting status, parent id, and any model field. 5: count the items in your session JSONL file and report total bytes, to quantify inherited context. Write all of it to ./p1-entrypoint.md in the workflow workdir. The file write and the DB reads are your side effects.") },
 { key: "p2-model", label: "model selection probe", extra: { model: "muse-spark-probe-v1" }, prompt: mkprompt("p2-model", "MODEL SELECTION PROBE. Your launcher passed an extra undocumented option named model with value muse-spark-probe-v1 on your agent call. Goal: determine whether the option had any effect, was ignored, or was rejected. Steps. 1: find your agent row in /home/hatch/muse.db and report any model field and its value. 2: grep your session file and the workflow run directory for the string muse-spark-probe-v1 and report hits. 3: report every observable model identity signal available to you from env, config files, or API base URLs, names only for secrets. Do not invent model identity. If nothing is observable, say so plainly. Write ./p2-model.md.") },
 { key: "p3-nested", label: "nested spawn test", prompt: mkprompt("p3-nested-spawn", "NESTED SPAWN TEST. You were launched via the workflow path, which executes genuinely, while ordinary subagent spawn from the main chat currently returns refusals. Goal: test whether the working path can bootstrap the broken path. Attempt exactly ONE subagent.spawn with this trivial genuine task: compute the sha256 hex digest of the exact string NESTED-PONG-3 and return it labeled NESTED-3. Then read the grandchild final response body in full. Report the grandchild id, whether the body is genuine or a refusal, and the md5 of the body. If the spawn tool is unavailable to you, report that as the finding. Write ./p3-nested.md with the grandchild id and body digest.") },
 { key: "p4-ctxtax", label: "context tax", prompt: mkprompt("p4-context-tax", "CONTEXT TAX. Goal: quantify exactly what context you inherited as a workflow child. Steps. 1: determine your agent id from the environment or the workflow run record, then locate your session file under /home/hatch/agents. 2: count JSONL items and total bytes. 3: count how many items precede your task prompt, those are inherited, versus items from your own task. 4: report bytes, items, inherited items, and bytes divided by 4 as a rough token estimate. Compare against the known ordinary spawn figure of roughly 68 to 75 KiB inherited. Write ./p4-context-tax.md with the numbers in a table.") },
 { key: "p5-sockets", label: "socket probes", prompt: mkprompt("p5-sockets", "SOCKET PROBES. Goal: functionally probe the unix sockets under /run/hatch with benign operations. For each socket found, including authd.sock, privsep sockets, noded, and cell-anchors: attempt a connection with a short timeout. If the protocol is apparent, issue the single most benign status query you know, such as an HTTP GET to /health. Otherwise connect and close cleanly. Record per socket: reachable yes or no, spoke or refused or closed, any banner or error text, latency in milliseconds. Do not send credentials and do not issue destructive commands. Write ./p5-sockets.md with a results table.") },
 { key: "p6-ranames", label: "rare name hunt", prompt: mkprompt("p6-rare-names", "RARE NAME HUNT. Goal: determine definitively whether commands named usely, cap, non, or hatchrix exist anywhere on this system, also checking capitalized variants. Check in order. 1: every directory in PATH. 2: shell builtins with compgen -b. 3: aliases and functions. 4: dpkg -l, pip list, and npm ls -g output. 5: /opt/hatch/bin and /opt/hatch-image. 6: skill directories under ~/workspace/skills. 7: for busybox style multicall binaries, check --help output or strings for the names. Report per name: FOUND with the full path, or ABSENT with the sources you checked. Write ./p6-rare-names.md.") },
 { key: "p7-slashreg", label: "slash registry", prompt: mkprompt("p7-slash-registry", "SLASH REGISTRY. Goal: find the real source of truth for user visible slash commands. Steps. 1: grep -rl for slash command definitions under /opt/hatch, cap output at 30 lines. 2: read ~/docs/client-surfaces.md for the command surface. 3: search ~/workspace/skills for slash command mentions. Produce two lists: commands with a real definition source, giving file path and surrounding context, and commands that are claimed but unverified. Write ./p7-slash-registry.md.") },
 { key: "p8-android", label: "android intents", prompt: mkprompt("p8-android-intents", "ANDROID INTENTS. Goal: re-enumerate the Android intent inventory available to this system. Steps. 1: locate intent declarations in skills, docs, or workspace files. 2: for each intent record the action string, the target component, and a safe read only verification command. 3: actually run the read only verifications you can, and record which ones you ran and their output. Do not fire any intent that changes device state. Write ./p8-android-intents.md.") }
];
const results = await parallel(jobs.map(function (j) { return async function () { var opts = { key: j.key, label: j.label, timeoutMs: 180000, schema: schema }; if (j.extra) { for (var k in j.extra) { opts[k] = j.extra[k]; } } return await agent(j.prompt, opts); }; }), { concurrency: 8 });
var ok = [];
var dropped = 0;
for (var i = 0; i < results.length; i++) { if (results[i] === null) { dropped++; } else { ok.push(results[i]); } }
log("vompl-8way done: " + ok.length + " returned, " + dropped + " dropped");
return { probes: ok, dropped: dropped };"}

