{"args":{"task":"TAU-18.2.2 FORENSIC RESTORE (awrawr-pc, via awrawr-mcp bridge). Chris's order: '18.2.2 for Tau is extremely important but that doesn't mean replace upstream of the folder'. Something/someone replaced the Tau folder's upstream contents with 18.2.2 material — that clobbering must be undone, and 18.2.2 must be integrated ADDITIVELY.

Steps: (1) Locate the Tau folder(s) on awrawr-pc: check /home/toxic/tau, ~/workspace/tauwork, /home/toxic/sovereign for tau dirs, plus any tau git repos. (2) Determine what '18.2.2' is: a version tag, a release tarball, a branch, a package version — find the actual artifact and its provenance. (3) Determine the folder's correct UPSTREAM state: git log / git reflog / remote origin; identify exactly which upstream files/commits were replaced or overwritten by the 18.2.2 drop. (4) Restore upstream contents forward-only: recover the replaced files from git objects, reflog, or origin (never force-push; if origin/main diverged, preserve origin/main as a backup branch first per standing rule). (5) Integrate 18.2.2 WITHOUT replacing upstream: side-by-side directory, versioned branch (e.g. tau-18.2.2), or additive overlay — upstream files stay byte-identical to upstream, 18.2.2 lives alongside. (6) Verify: diff upstream-tracked files against origin to prove zero clobbering; show where 18.2.2 now lives.

RULES: all shell via ~/workspace/bin/taskhook run -- <cmd>; on awrawr-pc use fd and rg (never find/grep); unique scratch dirs (never shared /tmp paths, never rm -rf shared dirs); NEVER git checkout a branch in a shared clone — use git worktree per task; additive only, never delete; push everything, never push to evmts/super-ralph; no fixed sleeps — poll observable conditions; time every major step at ms level. Read /home/toxic/.fleet-bus/directives.md before starting and report completion there per fleet convention. ALSO: register yourself in the new fleet coordination DB at /home/toxic/.fleet-bus/coord/ (see PROTOCOL.md there; if it does not exist yet, create your lane entry anyway as lanes/tau-18.2.2.json and heartbeat every few minutes). Deliver: what 18.2.2 is, what was replaced, what was restored (file list + SHAs), how 18.2.2 now coexists, verification diffs."},"name":"vompl"}

{"args":{"task":"FLEET COORDINATION SUBSTRATE PROBE + STANDUP. Chris's order: figure out what every agent can see and use that for coordination; awrawr-pc is the likely 'database holder' because all agents can reach it. You are the scout.

Steps: (1) VISIBILITY MAP. From your clean cell context, determine every shared read/write surface visible to fleet agents: (a) read ~/workspace/skills/awrawr-mcp/SKILL.md and test whether you can reach awrawr-pc through the bridge (run a trivial `echo` / `ls` through it); (b) via the bridge, check /home/toxic/.fleet-bus/directives.md — readable? writable? (c) check whether the cell ~/workspace is shared across agents (write a canary file to ~/workspace/.shingle-canary-<uuid>, read it back); (d) check /home/toxic/fleet/jobs and any other candidate shared paths. (2) STAND UP THE DATABASE HOLDER on awrawr-pc at /home/toxic/.fleet-bus/coord/: create PROTOCOL.md defining a minimal JSON-lines protocol (lanes/<lane>.json registry: agent id, lane name, task, heartbeat ts, status, blockers; claims/<item>.json for work-item claims; log.jsonl append-only event log), plus an empty log.jsonl. Keep it dead simple — files, no daemons. (3) ROUND-TRIP PROOF: from the cell, via the bridge, write a probe row into the coord DB and read it back; report both directions succeeded. (4) BROADCAST: append a dated entry to /home/toxic/.fleet-bus/directives.md announcing the coord DB path + protocol and instructing ALL fleet agents — including the four lanes currently running (tau-18.2.2 restore, cron/schedule resurrection, agent/subagent maximal repair, sovereign streaming project) — to register in lanes/ and heartbeat every few minutes.

RULES: all cell shell via ~/workspace/bin/taskhook run -- <cmd>; on awrawr-pc use fd/rg; additive only (never delete directives.md content — append dated entries newest-at-bottom); no fixed sleeps; time steps at ms level. Deliver: the definitive shared-visibility map (path -> readable/writable per agent class), the coord DB paths created, the round-trip proof, and the broadcast text you appended."},"name":"vompl"}

